   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to authorizing\ncertain premises, in the town of Rushford, county of Allegany, and within two\nhundred feet of a school, church, synagogue or other place of worship, to be\nlicensed to sell at retail, liquor and/or wine for off-premises consumption",
    "basePrintNo": "S8771",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8771-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §105, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8771\n\nSPONSOR: YOUNG\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nauthorizing certain premises, in the town of Rushford, county of Allega-\nny, and within two hundred feet of a school, church, synagogue or other\nplace of worship, to be licensed to sell at retail, liquor and/or wine\nfor off-premises consumption\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would establish an exemption from the alcoholic beverage\ncontrol law to authorize the state liquor authority to be able to disre-\ngard the 200-foot rule in the potential granting of an off premises wine\nand/or liquor license for a parcel of land in the town of Rushford,\nAllegany County.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of this bill amends subdivision 3 of section 105 of the alco-\nholic beverage control law to add a new paragraph (d) to provide an\nexemption from the 200-foot rule for a certain parcel of land containing\napproximately .08 acre in the town of Rushford, Allegany County.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe state liquor authority initially advised the owner of the Rushford\nGeneral store that the 200-foot rule would not apply to his application\nto operate a wine store license within the Town of Rushford. The rule\nspecifically prohibits the location of an off premises wine or liquor\nstore within 200 feet of a church. The First Baptist Church is within\n200 feet of the Rushford General Store. In reliance upon the information\nreceived from the liquor authority, the applicant made additional\nimprovements and investments in order to secure a wine/liquor off prem-\nises license for the Rushford General Store. Subsequently the state\nliquor authority admitted to the applicant that he was given incorrect\ninformation by the Authority; that the 200 foot rule would in fact bar\nhis application; and that he should pursue the introduction of special\nlegislation in order to be able to carry through with his plans. As a\nsmall business owner, the applicant wishes to ensure his store's contin-\nued survival in a small town which currently is experiencing a lack of\ncommercial activity.  In this instance, given that the applicant has\ndetrimentally relied upon information provided by the liquor authority,\nand in that the 200 foot rule is an antiquated vestige of the alcohol\nbeverage control law, it should not serve as an impediment to the expan-\nsion of a small rural business.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
